      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve been hesitating a bit whether we really should disallow zero-size walks, since there might be some potential use cases when a successful no-op would be actually the expected/desired behavior...\n\nIn particular, in `pkvm_guest_share_host()` and `pkvm_guest_unshare_host()` (unlike other memory protection API functions) we already allow size\u003d0, and I recall that I deliberately didn\u0027t add zero-size checks there, since I wasn\u0027t sure there are no cases when a guest kernel wants to share/unshare a zero-sized range (i.e. just do nothing and expect pKVM to return success in this case)...\n\nNow thinking more about it, probably there are no legitimate use cases for that, since at least, that would mean that the range address passed by the guest in those hypercalls makes no sense...\n\nAnd _probably_ in general we don\u0027t have and will not have legitimate use cases for zero-size pgtable walks in pKVM, so _probably_ disallowing them is a good idea...\n\nIf so, while we\u0027re at it, it may be also a good idea to \"fix\" `pkvm_guest_share_host()` and `pkvm_guest_unshare_host()` in this regard, i.e. add those missing zero-size checks to them?",
      "revId": "1ab0cab9399364b80008fcd840667292c17dcbdb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
